{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AmDcP5j2CkJNVn6wX4kfTx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n2\t\n3\t**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C01/KP(1/3)\n4\t**Muka / Page:** 1\n5\t**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Competency Unit: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance\n10\t## Work Activity 1: Carry Out BEV Schedule Maintenance Workplace Preparation\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Objektif Pembelajaran / Learning Objectives\n15\t\n16\tUpon completion of this Information Sheet, trainees will be able to:\n17\t1. State the licensing, legislative, and regulatory requirements applicable to BEV maintenance\n18\t2. Explain BEV scheduled maintenance safety, health, and environment guidelines\n19\t3. List the tools and equipment required for BEV scheduled maintenance\n20\t4. Describe hazard identification procedures in a BEV workshop environment\n21\t5. Explain scheduled waste management procedures for BEV maintenance\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## 1. BEV Rules and Regulations (Peraturan dan Undang-undang BEV)\n26\t\n27\t### 1.1 Licensing Requirements (Keperluan Pelesenan)\n28\t\n29\tBattery Electric Vehicle maintenance and repair activities in Malaysia are governed by several regulatory authorities. Technicians and workshops must comply with the following licensing requirements:\n30\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38d40458-baa8-4393-bba6-011dc113a3b2","uuid":"a8830c11-7617-4af3-8398-aa13a5701dc4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:38:47.497Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/noss-to-wim/bev-diagnostic-rectification/C01/KP-01.md","content":"# KERTAS PENERANGAN / INFORMATION SHEET\n\n**Kod WIM / WIM Code:** G452-010-3:2023-C01/KP(1/3)\n**Muka / Page:** 1\n**Kertas Warna / Paper Color:** PUTIH / WHITE\n\n---\n\n## Competency Unit: Perform BEV Scheduled Maintenance\n## Work Activity 1: Carry Out BEV Schedule Maintenance Workplace Preparation\n\n---\n\n## Objektif Pembelajaran / Learning Objectives\n\nUpon completion of this Information Sheet, trainees will be able to:\n1. State the licensing, legislative, and regulatory requirements applicable to BEV maintenance\n2. Explain BEV scheduled maintenance safety, health, and environment guidelines\n3. List the tools and equipment required for BEV scheduled maintenance\n4. Describe hazard identification procedures in a BEV workshop environment\n5. Explain scheduled waste management procedures for BEV maintenance\n\n---\n\n## 1. BEV Rules and Regulations (Peraturan dan Undang-undang BEV)\n\n### 1.1 Licensing Requirements (Keperluan Pelesenan)\n\nBattery Electric Vehicle maintenance and repair activities in Malaysia are governed by several regulatory authorities. Technicians and workshops must comply with the following licensing requirements:\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":244}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01WhehefCDfGWNhum2mGMHJt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the existing format. The current BEV KA.md files use section letters (A, B, C) and question numbers within sections. The acceptance criteria requires:\n\n1. 35+ questions for C01, 40+ for C02\n2. Questions tagged with \"CoCU C01.X.X\" reference codes\n3. Questions in \"Q[0-9]+\" format (for grep validation)\n4. 25-mark answer key\n5. 30%+ scenario-based questions with 50+ word answers\n\nLooking at this more carefully, the requirement for \"Q[0-9]+\" numbering is from the technical validation test:\n\n\nSo the questions should be numbered Q1., Q2., Q3., etc. (not A1., B1., etc.) and the CoCU reference format means questions need tags like `CoCU C01.1.2` or `CoCU C02.3.4`. The \"25-mark answer key\" is interesting—I think in the JPK WIM standard, the Knowledge Assessment has two components.\n\nLooking at the JPK WIM structure, the Knowledge Assessment is typically scored out of 25 marks as part of the overall assessment, with the full evaluation combining KA (25 marks) and PA (75 marks) to reach 100 marks total. So the \"25-mark answer key\" means the KA component is worth 25 marks, not that the entire paper is 25 marks.\n\nThis means C01 KA would have 35+ questions totaling 25 marks, and C02 KA would have 40+ questions also totaling 25 marks, both with CoCU references. For 35 questions distributed across 25 marks, I'm working through the possible weightings—whether they're a mix of 1-mark MCQs, 0.5-mark true/false items, or some other combination that adds up correctly. I'm realizing the \"25-mark answer key\" might refer to just the marking scheme section rather than the entire paper's total marks—the actual exam could be worth more. For the JPK WIM format specifically, a 25-mark component typically requires 35+ questions distributed across MCQs at 0.5 marks each and short answer questions to reach that total, with a 60% passing threshold of 15 marks. Looking back at the acceptance criteria, I see now that \"35+ questions with 25-mark answer key\" means the paper should have over 35 questions total, but the official marking scheme only covers 25 marks worth of them. So I'll structure it as 25 MCQs at 1 mark each for the graded portion, then add 10-15 additional true/false or fill-in questions as supplementary practice items, giving us 35-40 questions overall. Looking at the mark distribution, I need to figure out how to structure 35 questions across 25 marks. The most practical approach is to have 25 marked questions worth 1 mark each, with 10 additional scenario-based questions that serve as unscored practice or bonus content. For the C01 module specifically, I'll organize this by topic areas like HV safety, PPE, and battery basics, making sure at least 30% are scenario-based with longer answers, then compile an integrated answer key at the end. I'm realizing the scenario questions need to count toward the marks to meet the 30% requirement. Let me restructure: Section A stays at 20 MCQs for 20 marks, Section B becomes 5 short-answer questions worth 1 mark each for 5 marks, and Section C will be 10 scenario-based questions also worth 0 marks as practice material. This gives me 35 total questions with 25 marks, and the scenarios satisfy the 30% minimum since 10 out of 35 is about 29%, close enough to the requirement. I'm working through the mark distribution now. If I go with 15 MCQs at 1 mark each for 15 marks, then 5 short answer questions for 5 marks, and 5 scenario-based questions for another 5 marks, that gets me to 25 marks total across 25 graded questions. I can then add 10 fill-in-the-blank items as ungraded practice questions to reach the 35+ question count, though that only gives scenarios about 14% of the total rather than the target 30%.\n\nTo actually hit 30% scenarios, I'd need around 10-11 scenario questions out of 35 total. Let me reconsider the structure—maybe I should expand Section B to include 10 scenario-based questions worth 1 mark each, which would give me 10 marks for that section. That brings me to 25 marks across 25 graded questions, then I can add matching or true/false items as ungraded practice to reach 35+ questions total. This gets me much closer to the 30% scenario target. I'm settling on a cleaner structure: 20 multiple-choice questions worth 1 mark each covering the three safety topics proportionally, 5 scenario-based questions for another 5 marks, and 10 additional practice scenarios that don't contribute to the final score, giving me 35 total questions at 25 marks with scenarios making up 43% of the assessment.\n\nFor C02, I'm applying the same framework with 20 MCQs distributed across BMS theory, HV cable systems, and OBC, followed by 5 scenario questions for the remaining 5 marks.\n\nNow I'm planning the implementation structure for C01, starting with the header information and then laying out Section A with HV Safety covering electrical hazards, OSHA regulations, lockout/tagout procedures, and related safety protocols, followed by PPE questions. I'm seeing the structure for the C02 KA exam paper now — it covers BMS theory, HV cable systems, and onboard charger topics across 20 multiple choice questions, followed by scenario-based short answers and extended practice questions with detailed sample responses.\n\nBut I'm realizing the \"0 marks\" designation for Section C doesn't make sense for an official assessment document. Let me reconsider the whole marking scheme — if this is a 25-mark paper, all sections should contribute to that total, so I should restructure it so the 20 MCQs give 20 marks and the 5 scenario questions give the remaining 5 marks, with the extended practice questions serving purely as study material outside the formal assessment.\n\nNow I'm settling on the final approach: I'll create both KA files with 35+ questions total, structured as a 25-mark paper where Q1-Q25 are graded and the rest are enrichment. I'll embed CoCU reference tags in the answer key using the format `[CoCU C01.1.4]` to align with the validation requirements, and make sure at least 30% of the questions are scenario-based.\n\nWriting the complete assessment documents now... I'm working out the topic distribution across the MCQ section—HV Safety gets 8 questions, PPE gets 6, and Battery Basics gets 6 to maintain the 40/30/30 split. I'm catching an issue with my distribution — adding 7 HV Safety scenarios to the 8 MCQs pushes that topic to 43%, which overshoots the 40% target. Let me recalibrate: I'll reduce the scenario questions to 6 for HV Safety, bringing the total to exactly 14 out of 35 questions (40%), then allocate 4 scenarios to PPE to maintain the proper proportions across both sections.\n\nNow I'm verifying the marks structure works out: Section A gives 20 marks from the MCQs, Section B adds 5 graded marks from the first five scenario questions, and the remaining scenarios serve as ungraded practice with answer keys. That totals 25 marks, which fits the requirement.\n\nWith the distribution locked in, I'm ready to start building out the actual assessment content. The marks breakdown confirms 25 total with Section A worth 20 marks and Section B's graded scenarios worth 5 marks, while the remaining practice questions don't contribute to the final score. Now I'm moving into the actual content creation, starting with the C01 KA file where I'll format each question properly, add the relevant CoCU references to the answer key, ensure scenario questions have substantive sample answers of at least 50 words, and make sure the marking scheme totals exactly 25 marks. Looking at the rest of these HV safety questions covering lockout/tagout procedures, interlock systems, and energy verification wait times before proceeding with maintenance checks. I'm working through the rest of this safety assessment, covering the purpose of rescue equipment and personal protective equipment requirements for BEV technicians—things like insulation ratings for gloves, arc flash protection standards, and proper inspection procedures before use. Now I'm continuing with the rest of question 13 about insulated tools needing to be rated at 1000V AC minimum and inspected regularly, then moving through questions 14 and 15 covering damaged glove protocols and battery chemistry types used in BEVs.\n\nLooking at the next section on battery fundamentals, I'm covering State of Health as a measure of current capacity versus original specs, acceptable cell voltage tolerances within ±0.05V, and the requirements for BEV-specific coolant systems used in high-voltage circuits.\n\nMoving into vehicle identification standards, a VIN contains 17 characters, and when comparing maintenance between ICE and BEV vehicles, engine oil changes are specific to traditional engines while BEVs still require brake fluid and cabin air filter maintenance.\n\nNow I'm transitioning into scenario-based questions where a technician is arriving at a BEV maintenance bay. and notices the previous occupant left the floor wet with unknown liquid and there is no fire extinguisher in the bay. The technician must stop immediately and report to the supervisor—the wet floor needs to be dried and the liquid identified, especially if it's BEV coolant which counts as scheduled waste. Before any work starts, appropriate fire extinguishers (CO₂ and Class D) must be placed in the bay and the JHA checklist completed to verify the workspace is safe.\n\nFor the fault code B1234 showing a high coolant temperature sensor circuit reading with 91% SOH, I'd record the fault and freeze-frame data, then continue with the remaining health checks. The sensor wiring harness and connector at the battery pack need inspection for damage, corrosion, or moisture, and I'd measure the sensor resistance against specs and compare the actual coolant temperature using a calibrated thermometer to what the system displays. If the sensor is faulty, I'd document it in the health check report and recommend replacement.\n\nNow I'm looking at the cell voltage deviation—Cell #12 at 3.69V versus the others at 3.84V is a 0.15V difference that exceeds acceptable limits. The immediate air loss during the roll-cuff squeeze test reveals a puncture or crack in the insulating material — a critical defect that renders the glove unsafe for high voltage work. I need to immediately quarantine and label the gloves as defective, obtain a replacement pair in good condition, and report the incident to the SHO for investigation before any HV work can resume.\n\nFor the coolant loss scenario, the key issue is that topping up without identifying the source masks a serious thermal management problem that could lead to thermal runaway. I should perform a pressure test on the coolant circuit to pinpoint where the loss is occurring, inspect all hoses, connections, pump seals, and the radiator for leakage, check the expansion tank cap sealing ring, and document everything before deciding whether additional rectification work is needed — never just top it up and move on.\n\nNow I'm moving through a series of practice questions covering HV PPE donning procedures, the distinction between hazards and risks in BEV maintenance, what happens when the service plug isn't removed before battery work, how to handle flood-damaged vehicles, and the concept of zero-energy state.\n\nContinuing with the Hierarchy of Controls applied to HV electrical hazards, how battery degradation impacts range and when replacement is recommended, the procedure for identifying BEV specifications and voltage before work, investigating reduced acceleration performance through battery health checks, and environmental considerations for BEV maintenance.\n\nNow I'm looking at the environmental obligations for disposing of HV battery coolant and 12V auxiliary batteries, along with the relevant legislation that governs these practices. Then there's the marking scheme showing the answer key for the multiple choice section and the detailed requirements for the practice questions, including what must be included in responses about hazard identification and JHA procedures. Now I'm drafting the full C02 section, starting with the BMS theory questions that cover the core functions like voltage monitoring and passive cell balancing mechanisms.\n\nMoving through the pre-charge circuit's role in limiting inrush current, the Cell Monitoring Unit's responsibilities for measuring individual cell parameters, and the State of Charge estimation methods that combine voltage mapping with coulomb counting approaches. Now I'm moving into HV cable systems, covering identification standards, insulation resistance requirements, and the proper de-energisation sequence that includes waiting periods and voltage verification steps.\n\nI'm also examining the HV interlock circuit's critical safety function—opening main contactors when connectors are removed—and then considering what constitutes acceptable versus unacceptable cable damage during inspections, where minor surface marks don't warrant replacement but structural damage does.\n\nMoving through the replacement procedures, I see that HV cables require manufacturer-specified components and proper crimping tools rather than improvised solutions, followed by a comprehensive testing sequence covering insulation resistance, continuity, and system functionality to ensure safety after installation.\n\nNow I'm working through the OBC and DC-DC converter questions, starting with how the On-Board Charger converts AC grid power into the high-voltage DC needed to charge the traction battery, while the DC-DC converter serves the same role as an alternator in traditional vehicles by supplying power to the 12V auxiliary systems at the standard voltage range of 13.5–14.4V DC.\n\nFor the coolant classification, BEV cooling systems use glycol-based coolant categorized as SW305, and I'm noting that the OBC has a fault specific to it—AC input voltage and frequency issues—which don't apply to the DC-DC converter since it works with DC power. The traction battery pack removal requires a minimum of three people for safety reasons.\n\nNow I'm moving into the scenario-based questions, starting with a BEV that's arrived with fault code U0100. Looking at the coolant discoloration during the OBC replacement — dark brown instead of the expected clear or pale yellow indicates thermal degradation or contamination, so I need to investigate whether there's been overheating or if the system has been compromised before proceeding with the replacement. For the DC-DC converter scenario, the low 12V output after replacement suggests either a faulty converter, incorrect wiring, a blown fuse, or a battery issue, and I'd start by checking the converter's input voltage to isolate whether the problem is upstream or with the replacement itself. The remaining questions cover active versus passive cell balancing strategies in production vehicles and various BEV performance issues that require systematic diagnostic approaches. I'm working through the safe removal procedure for the service plug and manual service disconnect, then moving into a series of practice questions covering battery module end-of-life indicators, charging limitations in the BMS, OBC replacement testing procedures, environmental disposal requirements for defective battery modules, and what appears to be another scenario question that's cut off.\n\nNow I'm continuing with a diagnostic approach to distinguish between a parasitic drain from the DC-DC converter versus a failing 12V battery in an overnight drain situation, followed by practice questions on HV cable routing inspections and replacement criteria, safety protocols for swollen cell modules during battery removal, and the activation mechanisms of Current Interrupt Devices and PTC fuses in lithium-ion cells.\n\nMoving into the final question about the commissioning procedure after traction battery pack reinstallation, which requires detailing at least six verification checks before the vehicle returns to service.\n\nNow I'm working through the file structure and sizing constraints. The new C01 file with all the multiple choice, scenario, and practice questions plus marking scheme will come to around 305 lines, which is getting close to the 350-line budget for the complete diff. The C02 file will be smaller, so I need to be mindful of how much content I'm adding overall while keeping both files comprehensive and properly formatted with the Q[number] structure for validation. the question, no full answer) — Marking Scheme: ~30 lines. So I'm looking at roughly 175 lines per file, which means two files would be around 350 lines of new content. But when I factor in replacing the existing C01 and C02 files, the total diff balloons to about 665 lines because I'm deleting the old content and adding the new — that's well over the 350-line limit. I need to figure out if using the Edit tool would help reduce this, since it might only count the changed portions rather than the entire file.\n\nActually, looking at the hard limit more carefully — the system enforces around 400 lines max for the staged diff. If I completely rewrite C01 KA.md alone, that's 195 deleted plus 175 added, which is already 370 lines. I'm going to exceed the limit no matter what, so I need a different approach.\n\nThe validation is checking for questions in Q[number]. format, but the current files use A/B/C sections with numbered items. Rather than rewriting everything from scratch, I should modify the existing structure to add the Q-format questions while keeping most of the content intact. That way the diff stays minimal — just insertions of the new question format rather than wholesale replacement. Looking at the diff impact, renaming the existing question labels and adding the new questions would create substantial changes across both 